 "timestamp": 1794441600000}, "content-version": "stm-asf", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.15223/policy-029"}, {"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 11, 12]], "date-time": "2026-11-12T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1794441600000}, "content-version": "stm-asf", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.15223/policy-037"}, {"start": {"date-parts": [[2026, 11, 12]], "date-time": "2026-11-12T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1794441600000}, "content-version": "stm-asf", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.15223/policy-045"}], "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Although catalysis is widely exploited by nature to orchestrate noncovalent supramolecular assembly and build up complex living systems, its utilization in synthetic chemistry has conventionally been limited to accelerating covalent reactions. Consequently, the development of artificial systems for catalytic assembly remains a formidable and largely unexplored challenge. Here, we present a rationally designed strategy of electron-catalyzed supramolecular assembly, wherein the electron acts as an efficient, robust, and versatile catalyst to accelerate the formation of a donor–acceptor complex. Optimization of the elementary steps and intermediate stability yields an efficient catalytic cycle, furnishing the process with both a high turnover number and operational robustness in open air. Furthermore, the electron catalysis can be readily triggered by diverse external stimuli─including electricity, light, and mechanical force─enabling precise spatiotemporal control over the assembly process and near-quantitative synthesis of supramolecular complexes. Collectively, this work establishes a well-elucidated strategy of catalytic assembly with remarkable practicality, advancing the frontiers of noncovalent synthetic chemistry.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1021/jacs.6c16409", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 11, 12]], "date-time": "2026-11-12T23:01:57Z", "timestamp": 1794524517000}, "title": ["Electron-Catalyzed\nSupramolecular Assembly with High\nEfficiency, Robustness, and Versatility"], "author": [{"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6112-9092", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Guangcheng", "family": "Wu", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Northwestern University , , 2145 Sheridan Road , , ,", "place": ["Evanston, Illinois, United States, 60208"]}, {"name": "The University of Hong Kong , , ,", "place": ["Hong Kong SAR, China, 999077"]}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-3442-3466", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Qinghua", "family": "Yang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Northwestern University , , 2145 Sheridan Road , , ,", "place": ["Evanston, Illinois, United States, 60208"]}, {"name": "Northwestern University , , 2145 Sheridan Road , , ,", "place": ["Evanston, Illinois, United States, 60208"]}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9279-4714", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Yunyan", "family": "Qiu", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Northwestern University , , 2145 Sheridan Road , , ,", "place": ["Evanston, Illinois, United States, 60208"]}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"given": "Long", "family": "Zhang", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Northwestern University , , 2145 Sheridan Road , , ,", "place": ["Evanston, Illinois, United States, 60208"]}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8490-030X", "authenticated-orcid": true, "given": "Quentin", "family": "Stern", "sequence": "additional", "affiliation": [{"name": "Northwestern University , , 2145 Sheridan Road , , ,", "place": ["Evanston, Illinois, United States, 60208"]}], "role": [{"vocabulary": "crossref", "role": "author"}]}, {"ORCID": "https://orcid.org